      "math_academy_id": "q-64717",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14152724/assets/36bbd69dec59657a74992dbddced8b87190f953687e0d84aa8f3081e8754789c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that in the equations $y=\\pm \\operatorname{arccos}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14152724/assets/a8c4e910e3884ee68518752ab814f491edb14d422b4c25926b7d3ad34720592d.png)",
